\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, all four models are significant (\cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which pushes a linear probe to the noise floor and inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. Any composite metric that incorporates crossing rate is susceptible to this bias, and we hypothesize the effect generalizes beyond safety probing to any threshold-based detection study using crossing rate for layer selection. Validation on other probing tasks is needed to confirm generality.

\paragraph{Recommendations for probing methodology.} We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria, and that five-metric ablations (or at minimum, results at multiple candidate layers spanning shallow, middle, and deep positions) become standard practice. Even among unbiased metrics, the choice between threshold-based and threshold-free criteria affects layer selection: AUROC tends to select layers at shallower depth (3--50\%) than threshold-dependent metrics (44--98\%). Pure balanced accuracy exhibits the same pattern, selecting layers at 47--73\% depth for 32B models rather than the 94--98\% selected by threshold-dependent metrics. This divergence between threshold-free and threshold-dependent criteria further demonstrates that layer selection methodology is a critical researcher degree of freedom. At AUROC-optimal layers, HS probes retain directional precision advantage on all four models (+6.2 to +23.0pp), though these layers yield lower absolute performance than the deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics. The cost of ablation is modest: evaluating five metrics across all layers adds minutes to an experiment that takes hours. The alternative is that conclusions about representational structure reflect layer selection methodology rather than the phenomenon under study. The control task provides independent corroboration: R1-32B selectivity increases from 7.5pp at the biased layer (L10) to 19.7pp at the corrected layer (L63), which confirms that the biased metric selected layers where positional features dominate over genuine safety structure.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Information-theoretic reasoning suggests that hidden states (4096 to 5120 dimensions of internal computation) should dominate text features (384-dimensional sentence embeddings) purely by signal capacity. Both temporal context and modality choice significantly improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale. At 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds the temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}), so modality choice matters more than temporal context at this scale; at 32B, the relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Text probes benefit more from temporal context than HS probes because they start from a weaker single-step baseline. Safety commitment is gradual: the model progressively consolidates its intent over multiple reasoning steps rather than switching abruptly. The practical implication is that temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) is the most robust single default, as it provides guaranteed improvement regardless of modality, while the modality choice requires principled layer selection to realize its full potential.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence within identical architecture.}
R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the same base architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}). Training method (R1 distillation vs.\ native reasoning) is the likely differentiator, since architecture and scale are held constant. Per-model validation is essential: a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\paragraph{Design implications for safety monitoring.}
We recommend temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection (balanced accuracy or precision, never crossing rate) as deployment defaults. At 7--8B, HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). At 32B, practical trade-offs require model-specific decisions (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). All methods produce high safe-trace FPR (39--52\%), possibly because safe traces contain commitment-like state transitions (e.g., the model deciding to refuse) that probes cannot distinguish from harmful commitment. The 85.5\% oracle gap (\cref{sec:oracle}) confirms that passive monitoring captures a small portion of available signal; hidden states encode the model's current safety state but carry limited predictive power about future trajectory evolution. PCA dimensionality control and control task selectivity provide indirect evidence against textual leakage as the sole explanation for the HS advantage; direct erasure methods (e.g., LEACE~\citep{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.
